(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 27-year-old man in equatorial Africa presents with rapidly progressive violaceous nodules on both legs. He is not known to have human immunodeficiency virus infection and is not taking immunosuppressive therapy. Which clinical form of Kaposi sarcoma is described as often aggressive and potentially rapidly fatal in young Black men in equatorial Africa?","choices":["The iatrogenic form","The classic form","The endemic form","The epidemic form"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The endemic form is often aggressive disease in young Black men in equatorial Africa and can be rapidly fatal, in contrast to the classic form in older men, which follows a chronic course and is rarely fatal. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has folliculitis of the thighs that has failed to respond to antibacterial treatment, and a hair is plucked from an affected follicle. What test should be performed on it?","choices":["Direct immunofluorescence","A potassium hydroxide wet mount","A Wood's lamp examination","A Tzanck smear"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In resistant cases a potassium hydroxide wet mount using a plucked hair is used to rule out fungal, specifically dermatophyte, folliculitis. Culture and Gram stain from an unroofed pustule are also performed. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman is prescribed topical permethrin for scabies. What counselling should be given about the response to treatment?","choices":["Overnight to all skin; persistent itch means failure","Overnight to all skin; itch may persist for weeks","To visible areas only; resolves in 24 hours","Twenty minutes then rinse; no other measures"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Topical permethrin is applied overnight to the entire skin surface with special attention to creases. Most patients experience relief in about 3 days, but rash and pruritus may persist afterwards, which patients must be warned about or they will assume treatment failure. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has confirmed dermatophyte onychomycosis of two fingernails and four toenails. What is the usual duration of first-line oral terbinafine for each?","choices":["Six weeks fingernails, twelve weeks toenails","Twelve weeks fingernails, six weeks toenails","Two weeks for both","Lifelong therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oral terbinafine is first-line for most dermatophyte nail disease, usually 6 weeks for fingernails and 12 weeks for toenails, with hepatic disease and interactions reviewed and baseline liver tests obtained per labelling and patient risk. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a melanoma on the calf with a Breslow thickness of 1.4 mm and is being planned for definitive local treatment. What are the recommended re-excision margins for melanoma by thickness?","choices":["Margins set by lesion diameter","No re-excision after the biopsy","1 cm in situ, 2 cm under 1 mm, 3 cm over 1 mm","0.5 to 1 cm in situ, 1 cm under 1 mm, 1 to 2 cm over"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Definitive local treatment uses a 0.5 to 1 cm margin for in situ disease, 1 cm for tumours under 1 mm, and 1 to 2 cm for tumours over 1 mm. Expert-centre referral applies to melanoma deeper than 1 mm or with lymph node involvement. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man is about to be started on carbamazepine for newly diagnosed epilepsy. Which human leukocyte antigen association should prompt caution before prescribing it?","choices":["Human leukocyte antigen Cw6","Human leukocyte antigen B27","Human leukocyte antigen B*15:02","Human leukocyte antigen B*58:01"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Human leukocyte antigen B*15:02 is the risk allele associated with carbamazepine, while human leukocyte antigen B*58:01 is associated with allopurinol. Both are listed risk factors for Stevens-Johnson syndrome. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has symmetrical, well-defined, completely white non-scaly macules and patches on the dorsal hands and around the mouth. They fluoresce under a Wood's lamp. Her mother has autoimmune thyroid disease. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Vitiligo","Pityriasis versicolor","Leprosy","Postinflammatory hypopigmentation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vitiligo is an autoimmune disease causing depigmentation through T cell mediated destruction of melanocytes, producing asymptomatic white non-scaly macules and patches with distinct margins that fluoresce under a Wood's lamp, usually symmetrical. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with a lifetime of outdoor work has multiple 0.3 to 0.5 cm pink papules on the scalp and dorsal hands. Several are more apparent to touch than to sight. One lesion is thick, indurated, and has begun to bleed. What does that single lesion most require?","choices":["Biopsy","Field-directed therapy","Observation for one year","Reassurance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The most important distinction for actinic keratosis is early squamous cell carcinoma or carcinoma in situ, especially when a lesion is thick, indurated, ulcerated, enlarging, painful, bleeding, persistent, or recurrent. Those features change the lesion from one to treat destructively to one to sample. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with recurrent tinea pedis is being counselled on preventing further episodes. What patient education point is described as essential?","choices":["Sharing antifungal foot powder with household members","Walking barefoot in community showers to allow air drying","Drying between the toes after bathing","Soaking the feet daily in warm water"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Drying between the toes after bathing is marked as essential, alongside antifungal foot powder for shoes, wearing open-toed sandals when possible, using sandals in community showers, and changing socks frequently. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has an asymptomatic ring of flesh-coloured to pink papules on the dorsum of her hand that has slowly enlarged over months. There is no scale, and a potassium hydroxide preparation is negative. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea corporis","Granuloma annulare","Erythema multiforme","Sarcoidosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Localised granuloma annulare produces flesh-coloured to erythematous papules in an annular ring on the dorsal hands, feet, and ankles, and it is characteristically asymptomatic and without scale. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man presents with recurrent painful red nodules and draining lesions in both axillae and the groin. He has had four such episodes in the past six months and two lesions previously drained. He is obese and smokes. Examination shows inflamed nodules and several sinus tracts. What three elements establish the diagnosis?","choices":["Typical lesions, distribution, and biopsy","Typical lesions, axilla and groin, recurrence","One abscess with a family history","Typical lesions, distribution, positive culture"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hidradenitis suppurativa is primarily a clinical diagnosis requiring three key elements: typical lesions, characteristic distribution in the axilla and groin, and recurrence more than twice in six months. Biopsy is not usually required. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has fine red lines on both cheeks that disappear when pressed with a glass slide. How is a telangiectasia defined, and what is its behaviour on pressure?","choices":["A capillary proliferation forming a papule","A dilated capillary under 1 mm that does not blanch","A congenital malformation","A dilated capillary under 1 mm that blanches"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Telangiectasias are acquired vascular lesions consisting of permanently dilated capillaries under 1 mm. They blanch, may be single or grouped with a central punctum, may be primary or secondary, and are associated with numerous diseases. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has widespread tense bullae and a pruritic prodrome. The clinician plans a biopsy. What specimen and technique will best establish the diagnosis?","choices":["Patch testing for an allergen","Potassium hydroxide of blister fluid","Bulla centre for immunofluorescence alone","Lesion histology, perilesional immunofluorescence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bullous pemphigoid is diagnosed by biopsying the lesion for histopathology and perilesional tissue for direct immunofluorescence examination. Serum indirect immunofluorescence or enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay can identify anti-basement membrane zone antibodies. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has a warm, tender, erythematous area on the right lower leg with indistinct borders and surrounding oedema. What feature most reliably distinguishes cellulitis from erysipelas?","choices":["Cellulitis has indistinct, unraised borders","Only cellulitis produces fever","Only erysipelas is streptococcal","Cellulitis is bilateral, erysipelas unilateral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Erysipelas involves the upper dermis and superficial lymphatics, producing a plaque raised above the surrounding skin with a clear line of demarcation. Cellulitis involves the deeper dermis and subcutaneous tissue and therefore has borders that are indistinct and not raised. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has not been taking a medication prescribed for her three months ago and is reluctant to say why. Which barriers to treatment and medication adherence should be considered?","choices":["Cost, health literacy, and culture","Barriers cannot be anticipated","Cost of medications only","Patient motivation only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Barriers to treatment and medication adherence include the cost and affordability of medications or services, low health literacy or lack of understanding, and cultural or religious considerations. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 3-month-old infant with blistering at sites of minor friction is being investigated to establish where in the skin the split is occurring. What diagnostic test is considered the gold standard for establishing the level of cleavage in epidermolysis bullosa?","choices":["Skin biopsy with transmission electron microscopy","Genetic testing alone","Direct immunofluorescence for immunoglobulin A deposits","Immunofluorescence antigen mapping"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Skin biopsy with transmission electron microscopy is the gold standard for determining the cleavage plane. Immunofluorescence antigen mapping localises missing or reduced proteins, and genetic testing is confirmatory and guides prognosis and family counselling. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has a single asymptomatic annular ring of flesh-coloured papules on the dorsum of the foot. What course should be expected?","choices":["Lifelong immunosuppression is required","Progression to malignancy","Benign and self-limiting","Invariable recurrence after clearing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The localised variant accounts for about 75 percent of cases, presenting as flesh-coloured to erythematous papules in an annular ring on the dorsal hands, feet, and ankles, and is asymptomatic. Granuloma annulare is a benign, self-limiting granulomatous dermatosis. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"An 18-year-old boy is prescribed a topical antibiotic for acne, and benzoyl peroxide is added to the regimen. Why is benzoyl peroxide recommended alongside topical or oral antibiotic therapy for acne?","choices":["It is comedolytic and normalises keratinisation","It suppresses Cutibacterium and limits resistance","It inhibits tyrosinase and lightens pigment","It decreases sebum excretion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Benzoyl peroxide is keratolytic and anti-inflammatory and suppresses Cutibacterium acnes. It is recommended alongside antimicrobials specifically to reduce the risk of bacterial resistance, and combination products exist for that purpose. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man is found on routine examination to have several smooth, firm, deep red papules under 5 mm on the trunk that blanch with pressure. He has never noticed them and they are asymptomatic. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cherry angioma","Nevus araneus","Pyogenic granuloma","Petechiae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cherry angiomas are acquired vascular lesions formed by capillary proliferation, very common and increasing with age, appearing as smooth firm deep red papules under 5 mm on the trunk that blanch with pressure. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man presents with a single intensely itchy patch on the posterior neck that has been present for over a year. He describes rubbing it frequently, particularly when stressed or trying to fall asleep. Examination shows a well-demarcated thickened plaque with exaggerated skin markings, excoriation, scale, and altered pigmentation. What is the central principle of management?","choices":["Reassurance alone","Break the itch-scratch cycle","Indefinite potent topical corticosteroid","An oral antifungal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lichen simplex chronicus results from repeated rubbing and scratching, so management centres on breaking the itch-scratch cycle with education, treatment of the trigger, emollients, behavioural substitution, nail care, and safe physical occlusion, alongside a limited course of an appropriate potent topical corticosteroid. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 183"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has multiple smooth, slightly elevated, flat-topped, light-brown papules across her forehead and dorsal hands. She reports they seemed to multiply along the line where she shaves her legs. What form is this?","choices":["Molluscum contagiosum","Seborrheic keratoses","Flat warts","Verruca vulgaris"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Flat warts are multiple smooth, slightly elevated, flat-topped, skin-coloured to light-brown papules common on the face, forehead, dorsal hands, and shins, and shaving can spread lesions through autoinoculation. Observation is reasonable because spontaneous resolution is likely. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 143"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a translucent skin-coloured papule over the distal interphalangeal joint of her index finger, with a longitudinal groove in the adjacent nail. What is the nature of this lesion?","choices":["An overgrowth of dense fibrous tissue","An eccrine duct neoplasm","A pseudo-cyst of mucin extruded from the joint","A true cyst lined by epithelium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Digital mucous cysts are pseudo-cysts without a cellular lining or true capsule. They represent extrusion of mucinous contents from a local joint space into the surrounding dermis, typically over the distal interphalangeal joint, and may distort the nail. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has an intensely pruritic eruption of violaceous flat-topped papules on both wrists, and a biopsy is taken from one lesion. What histopathological finding is most characteristic?","choices":["Granulomas around degenerated collagen","Subepithelial blistering with neutrophils","A band-like lymphocytic infiltrate","Parakeratosis with neutrophilic microabscesses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy in lichen planus shows band-like infiltration of lymphocytes in the dermis. Additional characteristic findings include hyperkeratosis without parakeratosis, vacuolisation of the basal layer, Civatte bodies in the lower epidermis, and saw-tooth shaped rete ridges. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 177"},{"q":"A 6-year-old unvaccinated boy has a generalised itchy eruption concentrated on the trunk, scalp, and face. Macules, papules, vesicles, and crusts are all visible at the same time. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hand, foot and mouth disease","Scabies","Varicella","Herpes zoster"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Primary varicella-zoster virus infection causes a generalised pruritic eruption in multiple stages of healing, with macules, papules, vesicles, and crusts appearing simultaneously and lesions concentrated on the trunk, scalp, and face. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man with type 2 diabetes has an abrupt eruption of small pustules on the thighs and buttocks after wearing occlusive workout clothing. Each pustule sits on an erythematous base and is pierced by a central hair. He is afebrile. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Furunculosis","Acne vulgaris","Miliaria","Folliculitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Folliculitis is inflammation within the wall and ostia of the hair follicle, producing small papules and pustules on an erythematous base pierced by a central hair, most often from Staphylococcus aureus. Diabetes and occlusive clothing are listed risk factors. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man has maceration and erythematous erosions between the third and fourth toes with associated fissuring. What form of tinea pedis is this, and is it the commonest?","choices":["Interdigital tinea pedis, which is the most common form","Vesiculobullous tinea pedis, which is the most common form","Candidal intertrigo of the toe webs","Hyperkeratotic tinea pedis, which is the most common form"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Interdigital tinea pedis is the most common form, caused by Trichophyton species, producing maceration and erythematous erosions or scales between the toes, especially in the third and fourth interspaces, with associated fissures. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 10-year-old girl with red hair has multiple freckles across the nose and cheeks, and her mother asks what can be done about them. What is the cornerstone of management for ephelides?","choices":["Sun protection with patient education","Cryotherapy to each lesion","No management is possible","Surgical excision of each macule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sun protection is the cornerstone of management for ephelides, with proper patient education and counselling described as key. Topical depigmenting agents such as hydroquinone, retinoids, alpha-hydroxy acids, and botanicals may be used, and intense pulsed light is an option. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man is admitted with extensive flaccid bullae and painful erosions involving the mouth, chest, and groin. Biopsy demonstrates acantholysis and immunofluorescence confirms pathogenic antibodies. What is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Oral doxycycline with nicotinamide","Urgent treatment with rituximab or high-dose oral prednisone","Dapsone as single-agent therapy","Ultrapotent topical corticosteroids alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pemphigus is a life-threatening blistering disorder requiring urgent treatment, initially rituximab or high-dose oral prednisone. When steroids are used they are usually paired with a steroid-sparing agent such as azathioprine or mycophenolate so the patient can eventually be transitioned off. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman bitten by a brown recluse spider four days ago has a darkening lesion on the calf. How does a brown recluse bite evolve in the small percentage of cases that progress, and what governs the timing of surgery?","choices":["Necrosis at 2 to 3 days, eschar by day 7; delay surgery","Resolution without tissue loss","Systemic symptoms within 30 minutes","Full-thickness necrosis within hours; debride now"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a small percentage of cases the initial wound progresses to necrosis 2 to 3 days after the bite, then eschar formation between days 5 and 7, then deep ulcers. Necrotic wounds heal very slowly and may need surgical intervention or reconstruction, with surgery delayed. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy has lesions on the face, upper back, and chest for a year. Examination shows open and closed comedones together with inflammatory papules and pustules, and a few nodules. There is no flushing and no telangiectasia. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Perioral dermatitis","Acne vulgaris","Acne rosacea","Folliculitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acne vulgaris is polymorphic, with comedones as the hallmark lesion alongside papules, pustules, and nodules, affecting areas with hormonally responsive sebaceous glands — the face, neck, upper arms, back, and chest. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man taking allopurinol for three weeks develops fever, malaise, and painful swallowing, followed by erosions of the lips, mouth, and conjunctivae, and dusky skin lesions with detachment involving about 6 percent of his body surface area. What is the single most important immediate action?","choices":["Give systemic corticosteroids","Obtain a skin biopsy","Admit to a burn unit","Withdraw all suspect medications"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Immediate causative drug withdrawal is the first step, and earlier withdrawal is strongly associated with improved survival — each day of delay worsens prognosis. Allopurinol is named as the most common trigger worldwide. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"A 12-year-old girl presents with fever and headache four days after a tick bite, and a rash has now appeared. What is the clinical triad of Rocky Mountain spotted fever, and how often is it present?","choices":["Fever, headache, and rash, in nearly all","Fever, arthritis, and erythema migrans","Rash, pruritus, and lymphadenopathy","Fever, headache, and rash, in about 60 percent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The clinical triad is fever above 39.5 degrees Celsius, headache, and rash, present in only about 60 percent of patients. Fever presents in the first 3 days with rash following 2 to 4 days after fever onset. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 44-year-old fair-skinned woman newly diagnosed with rosacea asks which trigger is most important to control. What is the most accurate answer?","choices":["Sun exposure, described as the most universal trigger","Topical corticosteroid use, which is the primary cause","Alcohol, described as the most universal trigger","Dairy products, which drive Demodex overgrowth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sun exposure is named as the most universal trigger of rosacea, alongside heat, alcohol, spicy foods, and stress. Chronic ultraviolet exposure is also a listed risk factor. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has symmetrical, well-defined white non-scaly macules and patches on the dorsal hands and around the mouth. They fluoresce under a Wood's lamp. What is the underlying mechanism?","choices":["T cell mediated autoimmune destruction of melanocytes","Postinflammatory loss of pigment after a dermatitis","Reduced melanin production by intact melanocytes","Overgrowth of Malassezia altering melanocyte function"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vitiligo is a common autoimmune disease causing depigmentation through T cell mediated destruction of melanocytes. It can begin at any age but usually starts before the thirties, with half of patients presenting before their twenties. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"The mother of a 3-year-old boy with atopic dermatitis asks how to apply the emollient she has been given. What instruction is correct?","choices":["Apply after rinsing; avoid fragrance","Bathe in hot water first","Withhold on steroid days","Apply only to inflamed areas"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prevention advice is that emollient should be applied to the skin after rinsing, the patient should avoid irritants, and skin care should be fragrance-free. Clinicians should also demonstrate medication quantity and correct application. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl has fifteen discrete, smooth, firm, flesh-coloured dome-shaped papules averaging 4 mm on the trunk and arms. Each has a central dimple. They are asymptomatic. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Verruca vulgaris","Folliculitis","Molluscum contagiosum","Basal cell carcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Molluscum contagiosum is a benign poxvirus infection producing discrete, smooth, firm, flesh-coloured dome-shaped pearly papules averaging 3 to 5 mm, and central umbilication is characteristic. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 134"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man presents two days after clearing brush at a summer job. He reports intense itching. Examination shows erythematous papules, vesicles, and a few small bullae arranged in linear streaks across the forearms and one calf, with excoriation between them. Some lesions appear newer than others. He is afebrile and the rash does not cross the midline of the trunk or follow a single band. What feature of the eruption best supports the diagnosis?","choices":["The onset within two days of the exposure","The linear arrangement of the lesions","The presence of vesicles and bullae","The intensity of the pruritus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Urushiol sap from Toxicodendron species produces intensely pruritic vesicular lesions in linear distributions, because the streaks mark where the plant grazed the skin in passing. Lesions erupt within 4 to 96 hours and may appear in multiple stages, as they do here. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man develops an extremely pruritic papulovesicular eruption on the fingers ten days after being diagnosed with tinea pedis. A potassium hydroxide preparation from the finger lesions is negative, while one from the toe webs is positive. What three criteria establish this diagnosis?","choices":["Positive Wood's lamp, negative scraping, biopsy","Dermatophyte elsewhere, none at the site, resolves","Dermatophyte elsewhere, fungal elements at the site","Positive bacterial culture, responds to antibiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Those are the three criteria for establishing an id reaction. The primary site is potassium hydroxide positive and the distant id site is potassium hydroxide negative, and the eruption occurs 1 to 2 weeks after the primary infection. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with hidradenitis suppurativa who smokes and is obese asks what she can do herself to reduce flares. Which measure is described as essential?","choices":["Wearing tight-fitting garments to support the affected areas","Smoking cessation","Daily vigorous scrubbing of the affected areas","Shaving the affected areas daily with a multi-blade razor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Smoking cessation is described as essential among the preventive measures, alongside avoiding heat exposure, daily cleansing with an antibacterial soap or chlorhexidine or benzoyl peroxide wash, weight loss if obese, and avoiding constrictive clothing and frictional trauma. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man presents with a new benign lesion on a sun-exposed area of the forearm. What opportunity does this create?","choices":["Reassurance alone","Referral for full-body photography","Advising complete avoidance of outdoors","Counselling on sunscreen and self-examination"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When discussing any new lesion in sun-exposed areas, the clinician should take the opportunity to counsel on sunscreen use, avoiding direct sun during peak hours, and performing periodic skin self-examinations. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man develops a prickling sensation lasting about 30 minutes after wading in a freshwater lake, followed 10 to 12 hours later by severe itching and then erythematous papules on the legs. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cutaneous larva migrans","Cercarial dermatitis","Contact dermatitis","Folliculitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cercarial dermatitis, or swimmer's itch, follows penetration of the skin by cercarial forms of parasitic flatworms, producing urticaria-like lesions and a prickling sensation lasting about 30 minutes after exposure, severe pruritus at 10 to 12 hours, and erythematous papules within 24 hours. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman is to begin oral isotretinoin for recalcitrant nodular acne. What safety requirements apply?","choices":["Monthly liver tests, no pregnancy testing","A pregnancy test before starting only","No specific requirements","Tests before, monthly, and 5 weeks after"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Isotretinoin is a potent teratogen. Negative pregnancy tests are mandatory prior to initiation, monthly, and 5 weeks post treatment, only one month is dispensed at a time, and providers must be registered. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has recurrent grouped bites appearing each morning after sleeping in her own bed. What is essential in the management of a bedbug infestation beyond treating the patient's skin?","choices":["Washing bedding at 60 degrees Celsius alone","No environmental action","A professional exterminator","Treating all contacts with permethrin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Management of the patient is symptomatic with local wound care, topical antiseptic or antibiotic cream for secondary infection, and topical corticosteroids or oral antihistamines for pruritus. A professional exterminator is necessary to eradicate the infestation itself. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has a hard, conical keratotic projection on the dorsum of his hand. What is the key principle in evaluating this lesion?","choices":["Diagnose the lesion at the base","A conical shape means benign","Send the horn; leave the base alone","Cryotherapy without histology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A cutaneous horn arises from the surface of another lesion, which may be benign or malignant — actinic keratosis, warts, and squamous cell carcinoma among them. There is often no clinical feature to distinguish them, so the underlying lesion must be diagnosed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man is diagnosed with a melanoma on the upper back that had spread radially for months before entering a vertical growth phase. What is the most common subtype of melanoma?","choices":["Superficial spreading melanoma","Lentigo maligna melanoma","Amelanotic melanoma","Acral lentiginous melanoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Superficial spreading melanoma accounts for about two-thirds of cases, arises on intermittently sun-exposed skin, and evolves radially before entering vertical growth. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has a firm 8 mm nodule on the lower leg with a brown halo and a slightly raised centre. Squeezing the skin on either side makes the lesion dimple downward. It occasionally hurts when knocked. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Keratoacanthoma","Melanoma","Dermatofibroma","Hypertrophic scar"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dermatofibroma is a firm 0.5 to 1 cm nodule of dense dermal fibroblasts, most common on the legs, with a hyperpigmented halo and a positive dimple sign. It is the most common painful skin tumour. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman has a soft, mobile, non-tender subcutaneous mass on the upper back that has been slowly enlarging for years. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Liposarcoma","Cutaneous abscess","Epidermoid cyst","Lipoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A lipoma is a benign localised overgrowth of fat cells in subcutaneous tissue and the most common soft tissue tumour. Diagnosis is typically clinical, and asymptomatic tumours can be observed, with excision considered for cosmetically deforming or enlarging lesions. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man has a crusted genital lesion that has been present for six days. What is the preferred diagnostic test for a suspected herpes simplex virus lesion, and how should a negative result on an older lesion be interpreted?","choices":["Viral culture; a negative result excludes infection","Nucleic acid amplification from a fresh lesion","A Tzanck smear, which types the virus","Immunoglobulin M serology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Swabbing a fresh vesicle, ulcer base, or crust for type-specific nucleic acid amplification testing is the preferred test. A negative swab from an older lesion does not exclude infection because shedding is intermittent, and culture is less sensitive in healing or recurrent lesions. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has sudden fever to 39 degrees Celsius with chills, followed within a day by a painful red area on the left shin. Examination shows a brightly erythematous plaque raised above the surrounding skin with a sharply defined border and red streaks running proximally. She has tinea pedis. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Stasis dermatitis","Necrotizing fasciitis","Erysipelas","Cellulitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Erysipelas involves the upper dermis and superficial lymphatics, producing a plaque raised above the surrounding skin with a clear line of demarcation, sudden high fever, and lymphatic streaking. Tinea pedis is a recognised portal of entry. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 12-year-old has several elevated, round papules under 1 cm with a rough greyish surface on the fingers. Trimming one reveals tiny red-black dots. What do those dots represent, and what does their presence help exclude?","choices":["Deep roots, confirming the need for excision","Thrombosed capillaries, distinguishing it from a callus","Melanocytic nests, excluding melanoma","Keratin plugs, distinguishing it from a keratosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tiny red or black dots represent thrombosed dilated capillaries, and trimming the surface makes them more prominent. A callus lacks them, which is why paring the lesion is a useful bedside manoeuvre. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 142"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man presents with a rash that began on the backs of his hands and spread toward the trunk over two days. Examination shows numerous lesions with three concentric zones — a dusky centre, a pale ring, and an erythematous outer rim — on the dorsal hands, forearms, and knees. Individual lesions have remained fixed in place for four days. He had a cold sore on his lip ten days ago. Less than 5 percent of his body surface is involved and there are no mucosal erosions. What is the most likely trigger?","choices":["A sulfonamide antibiotic","Mycoplasma pneumoniae","No identifiable trigger","Herpes simplex virus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Over 50 percent of erythema multiforme cases are triggered by herpes simplex virus, making it the most common precipitant, and a history of recurrent infection is a listed risk factor. The cold sole ten days before the eruption supplies exactly that history. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy has eight light brown macules greater than 5 mm and freckling in both axillae. What condition does this suggest, and what is the freckling called?","choices":["Postinflammatory hyperpigmentation","Neurofibromatosis type 1; Crowe's sign","Neurofibromatosis type 2","Multiple lentigines from sun exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Café au lait spots are often the first manifestation of neurofibromatosis type 1, defined as light tan to brown macules over 5 mm prepubertally. Intertriginous freckling in the axillary and inguinal regions is Crowe's sign, with freckles smaller than 5 mm. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has several beige to dark brown papules and plaques 5 to 15 mm on the trunk that feel velvety and appear pasted onto the skin surface. They have appeared gradually over years. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Malignant melanoma","Melanocytic naevus","Seborrheic keratosis","Actinic keratosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Seborrheic keratoses are benign papules and plaques, beige to brown to black, 2 to 20 mm, feeling velvety or warty and appearing stuck or pasted onto the skin. They are common in older adults and easily mistaken for neoplasms. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman has an intensely itchy eruption of small flat-topped shiny violaceous papules on both volar wrists and ankles, some coalescing into plaques with fine white surface lines. She also has lacy white streaks inside her cheeks. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dermatitis herpetiformis","Psoriasis","Lichen simplex chronicus","Lichen planus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lichen planus produces intensely pruritic purple polygonal papules and plaques, classically at the ankles and volar wrists, with Wickham striae on the surface. Oral disease may show erosive lesions or the same striae. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 176"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has a biopsy-proven keratoacanthoma on the dorsal forearm. What is the standard of care, and what margin is used for elliptical excision?","choices":["Excision with 5 mm margins","Intralesional corticosteroid","Observation alone","Cryotherapy without histology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The standard of care is to excise or destroy the tumour, preferred because of possible malignancy, with elliptical excision using 5 mm margins. Mohs surgery may be indicated for large or recurrent lesions or those in cosmetically sensitive locations. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy has scaly patches of hair loss, and the clinician suspects a fungal or bacterial infection producing pigment change and reaches for a handheld device emitting long-wave ultraviolet light. What is this device and what does it evaluate?","choices":["A trichoscope, magnifying the scalp","A dermoscope, magnifying under polarised light","A phototherapy unit delivering ultraviolet B","A Wood's lamp, evaluating pigment change"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A Wood's lamp is a handheld diagnostic device emitting long-wave ultraviolet light to highlight subtle changes in the skin, scalp, and hair, and it evaluates pigment changes in selected fungal or bacterial infections. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman reports generalised itching every winter with rough, flaking skin worst on the shins. Examination shows dry skin with fine scale and superficial fissuring, without erythematous plaques, vesicles, or burrows. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nummular eczema","Atopic dermatitis","Xerosis","Scabies"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Xerosis reflects impaired stratum corneum hydration promoted by aging, low humidity, hot water, and detergents. It commonly affects older adults in winter and causes tightness, pruritus, rough scale, and fissuring. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 152"},{"q":"A clinician evaluating a 58-year-old man for a condition that would be dangerous to miss wants a test that will correctly identify patients who do have the condition, minimising the number of affected patients missed. What property is she describing?","choices":["Posttest probability","Sensitivity","Specificity","Pretest probability"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sensitivity is the probability that the test shows a person has the condition when they do have it — how well a test detects a condition. Sensitivity is related to fewer false negatives. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has a sharply bounded eruption on the forearm whose outline does not correspond to any anatomical distribution. What general feature most often allows contact dermatitis to be distinguished from other dermatologic conditions on inspection alone?","choices":["Its predilection for sebum-rich areas of the body","Its symmetric involvement of flexural surfaces","Its clearly demarcated, unnatural patterns","Its tendency to produce lesions with central clearing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Contact dermatitis in general is often distinguishable from other dermatologic conditions because of its clearly demarcated, unnatural patterns — the eruption traces the shape of whatever touched the skin rather than following a biological distribution. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 15-year-old girl presents with comedones, papules, and pustules of the face and upper back. What are the four factors involved in the pathogenesis of acne vulgaris?","choices":["Apocrine occlusion, bacteria, sinus tracts, scarring","Hyperkeratinisation, sebum, Cutibacterium, inflammation","Autoimmune folliculitis and androgen excess","Neurovascular dysregulation and Demodex overgrowth"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acne involves follicular hyperkeratinisation with excess keratin in the follicle, increased sebum production providing a growth medium, proliferation of Cutibacterium acnes, and inflammation. The temporal sequence among them is not fully understood. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 6-year-old unvaccinated child has a generalised pruritic eruption concentrated on the trunk, scalp, and face. Macules, papules, vesicles, and crusts are all present at the same time. What feature is most characteristic?","choices":["Lesions confined to a single dermatome","Lesions in multiple stages of healing appearing simultaneously","Lesions limited to the palms and soles","Lesions all at the same stage of evolution"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Primary varicella-zoster virus infection causes a generalised pruritic eruption in multiple stages of healing — macules progress to papules, vesicles, and crusts, with several stages appearing simultaneously. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 29-year-old fair-skinned woman asks how to use sunscreen correctly before a beach holiday. What advice matches the guidance given?","choices":["Only for Fitzpatrick types I to III","Factor 30 daily, reapplied every 2 hours","Factor 15, reapplied only after swimming","Broad-spectrum factor 30 daily, applied once"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Broad-spectrum sun protection factor 30 or above is advised for daily use and 50 or above for prolonged outdoor exposure, applied 15 minutes before sun exposure and reapplied every 2 hours. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A newborn boy has a large flat brown plaque with a slightly uneven border and a pebbly surface over the posterior scalp and upper back, present at delivery. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cafe au lait macule","Congenital melanocytic naevus","Nevus spilus","Dermal melanocytosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Congenital melanocytic naevi arise from somatic mutations and are evident at birth or shortly after, presenting as flat brown patches or plaques with smooth or slightly uneven borders and sometimes a pebbly, rugose, or verrucous surface. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy has scaly patches on the scalp with areas of hair loss. Examination shows grey ring-formed patches with perifollicular papules and visible black dots where hairs have fractured at the surface. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrheic dermatitis","Tinea capitis","Trichotillomania","Psoriasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tinea capitis is the most common fungal infection in children, producing red papules progressing to greyish ring-formed patches with perifollicular papules, scaly patches, and alopecia. Black dot tinea capitis reflects fracture of the hair. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man develops severe nocturnal itching six weeks after his partner was treated for scabies. How is scabies typically transmitted, and how long after infestation does pruritus appear?","choices":["Close contact 15 to 20 minutes; itch at 4 weeks","Brief casual contact; itch within 24 hours","Airborne spread; itch after 1 week","Head-to-head contact; itch at 4 to 6 weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Scabies is transmitted by close physical contact for 15 to 20 minutes and may also be acquired from the bedding or underclothing of an infested individual. Pruritus appears 4 to 6 weeks after initial infestation, and many patients do not develop symptoms for up to 3 months. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"}]};
